CH = dict(num=4, title="Changes Around Us: Physical and Chemical", theme="Physical vs chemical change, combustion, weathering",
intro="Ice melts, buds bloom, bananas turn brown and cold water warms up: changes are everywhere. This chapter sorts changes into physical and chemical, studies rusting and combustion, looks at the fire triangle, shows that one process can involve both kinds of change, and ends with reversible and irreversible changes, desirable and undesirable changes, and slow natural changes like weathering and erosion.",
big_questions=["How can we tell whether a new substance has formed?", "What three things are needed for fire?", "Is burning a candle a physical or a chemical change?", "How are rocks changed into soil?"],
blocks=[
("h2", "5.0 Changes we observe"),
("p", "Activity 5.1 lists changes such as melting ice, chopping vegetables, boiling water, making popcorn, cutting paper, adding beetroot extract to water, burning wood, drying clothes, making dough balls and rolling chapatis. Each causes a change in size, shape, smell, colour, state or another property. We notice changes through our senses; the aim is to arrange them into categories."),
("h2", "5.1 A Substance May Change in Appearance but Remain the Same"),
("p", "Activity 5.2: folding paper into shapes, inflating and deflating a balloon, and crushing chalk into powder. In each case the material (paper, rubber, chalk) stays the same even though shape or size changes. Water too changes between solid, liquid and gas but remains water."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "A **physical change** is a change in physical properties (shape, size, state, etc.) in which **no new substance is formed**."),
("h2", "5.2 A Substance May Change in Appearance and Not Remain the Same"),
("steps", [("Prepare", "Take two tumblers: A with a little tap water, B with lime water."), ("Blow", "Gently exhale through a straw into each (never suck the liquid)."), ("Observe", "A: only bubbles, no change. B: lime water turns **milky**; on standing a white solid settles. Something new has formed.")]),
("p", "Exhaled **carbon dioxide** reacts with lime water (calcium hydroxide) to form **calcium carbonate**, a white solid insoluble in water, plus water."),
("note", "FORMULA", "Calcium hydroxide (lime water) + Carbon dioxide &rarr; Calcium carbonate (insoluble) + Water"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "A **chemical change** is one in which **one or more new substances are formed**. The process is a **chemical reaction**, written as a **chemical equation**. Milkiness of lime water is the **test for carbon dioxide**."),
("h3", "Vinegar and baking soda (Activity 5.4)"),
("ul", ["Add a pinch of baking soda (sodium hydrogen carbonate) to vinegar or lemon juice: fizzing and gas bubbles.", "Pass the gas through fresh lime water: it turns milky, so the gas is **carbon dioxide**.", "Vinegar + Baking soda &rarr; Carbon dioxide + Other substances: a chemical change.", "Baking soda in plain water gives no bubbles."]),
("h2", "5.3 Other Processes Involving Chemical Changes"),
("h3", "5.3.1 Rusting"),
("p", "Rust, a brown substance (iron oxide), is formed when iron meets air and water, so rusting is a chemical change."),
("h3", "5.3.2 Combustion"),
("p", "Burning magnesium ribbon forms magnesium oxide with heat and light, so it is a chemical change."),
("note", "FORMULA", "Magnesium + Oxygen &rarr; Magnesium oxide (white powder) + Heat + Light"),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Combustion** is a chemical reaction in which a substance reacts with oxygen to give heat and/or light. Substances that burn are **combustible** (wood, paper, cotton, kerosene)."),
("h3", "Is oxygen needed? (Activity 5.5)"),
("p", "Two identical candles are lit; one is covered by a glass tumbler. The covered one goes out after a while because air (oxygen) is cut off. Lime water added in the tumbler turns milky: carbon dioxide formed from wax carbon and oxygen of air. **Oxygen is essential for combustion.**"),
("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Putting out clothes on fire:** wrap the person in a blanket or cloth to cut off air. Never use synthetic cloth: it melts and sticks to skin."),
("ul", ["**Bioluminescence:** fireflies emit light (without heat) by a chemical change."]),
("h3", "Is air enough? Ignition temperature (Activity 5.6)"),
("p", "A lit match sets paper on fire instantly. Focusing sunlight with a magnifying glass also makes paper smoke and burn, so fire is not needed to start burning; heat is. The paper warms until it reaches its **ignition temperature**."),
("note", "DEFINITION", "**Ignition temperature** is the minimum temperature at which a substance catches fire."),
("table", ["Requirement for combustion (fire triangle)", "Meaning"], [["Combustible substance (fuel)", "Something that can burn"], ["Oxygen", "Supplied by air"], ["Heat", "Raises the fuel to its ignition temperature"]]),
("p", "Removing any side of the triangle puts out the fire."),
("h2", "5.4 Can Physical and Chemical Changes Occur in the Same Process?"),
("p", "In a burning candle the wax **melts** (physical), rises through the wick, **evaporates** (physical), and the **wax vapour burns** (chemical). Solidifying of dripping wax is physical. So the burning of a candle involves both kinds of change."),
("p", "**Know a scientist:** Michael Faraday gave the nineteenth-century lectures *Chemical History of a Candle*, using a candle to teach melting, vapourisation and combustion."),
("h2", "5.5 Are Changes Permanent?"),
("table", ["Reversible (original can be obtained)", "Irreversible (cannot be brought back)"], [["Melting ice (refreezes), boiling water (steam condenses), dissolving sugar in water, folding a mat, twisting string", "Chopping vegetables, making popcorn, cooking, idli from batter, grinding wheat, ripening fruit, curdling milk, burning, rusting, formation of soil"]]),
("note", "NOTE", "Physical changes are often reversible, but not always (chopping, grinding). Chemical changes are generally irreversible."),
("h2", "5.6 Are All Changes Desirable?"),
("ul", ["**Desirable:** milk to curd, fruit ripening, cooking food, cutting fruit.", "**Undesirable:** rusting of iron, decay of stored food. A change can be good in one setting: decomposition of food waste makes **compost**.", "**Long-term harm from human activity:** burning more fuel in vehicles raises carbon dioxide in the air; drying paint releases substances that pollute the air."]),
("h2", "5.7 Some Slow Natural Changes"),
("h3", "5.7.1 Weathering of rocks"),
("ul", ["Heaps of sand, soil and stones at the base of mountains are **sediments**.", "**Physical causes:** temperature changes, growing tree roots, water freezing in cracks.", "**Chemical causes:** water or chemicals in water react with rocks, e.g. black iron-containing basalt gets a red layer of **iron oxide**.", "Physical and chemical breaking of rocks together is **weathering**, which finally forms **soil**."]),
("h3", "5.7.2 Erosion"),
("ul", ["**Erosion** is the breaking down and movement of rocks, pebbles, soil and sediments by wind and flowing water.", "River pebbles are smooth because of constant erosion; landslides are an example (physical change).", "When water or wind slows (lake, ocean), material settles, and the sediments harden over time into **new rocks**.", "These changes take thousands of years and are not reversible."]),
("table", ["Feature", "Physical change", "Chemical change"], [["New substance", "No", "Yes"], ["What changes", "Shape, size, state, appearance", "Composition and properties"], ["Reversibility", "Often reversible", "Usually irreversible"], ["Energy", "Usually little", "Often heat, light, gas, colour or smell"], ["Examples", "Melting, tearing, folding, dissolving sugar", "Rusting, burning, curdling, ripening, baking soda + vinegar"]]),
("h2", "Likely exam questions"),
("qa", [("Define physical and chemical change with examples.", "Physical: no new substance (melting ice). Chemical: new substance formed (rusting)."),
("How do you test for carbon dioxide?", "Pass it through fresh lime water; it turns milky."),
("What is formed on exhaling into lime water?", "White calcium carbonate, so the lime water turns milky."),
("What is combustion?", "Reaction of a substance with oxygen producing heat and/or light."),
("What are the three requirements for combustion?", "Fuel, oxygen and heat to reach ignition temperature (fire triangle)."),
("Define ignition temperature.", "The lowest temperature at which a substance catches fire."),
("Why does a covered candle go out?", "Air (oxygen) is cut off."),
("Is the burning of a candle physical or chemical?", "Both: melting and evaporation of wax are physical; burning of vapour is chemical."),
("Is curdling of milk physical or chemical?", "Chemical; a new substance (curd) with different properties forms and cannot be reversed."),
("Are ice to water and water to steam chemical?", "No, both are physical changes of state; no new substance is formed."),
("Differentiate weathering and erosion.", "Weathering is physical/chemical breaking of rocks to form soil; erosion is breaking down and carrying away of material by wind or water."),
("Why is rusting a chemical change?", "A new brown substance, iron oxide, forms.")]),
("h2", "Exploratory projects"),
("ul", ["Observe the kitchen and list reversible changes.", "Yeast project: sugar solution + yeast in a bottle with a balloon on top gives a gas that turns lime water milky (carbon dioxide).", "Lemon-juice invisible ink: writing appears brown on warming (a chemical change).", "Discuss steps to reduce landslides and rock erosion."]),
],
takeaways=["**Physical change:** no new substance; **chemical change:** new substance(s) formed through a chemical reaction.", "Lime water turning milky tests for **carbon dioxide**.", "**Combustion** needs fuel, oxygen and heat (reaching **ignition temperature**) and gives heat and/or light.", "Rusting, burning, cooking, curdling are chemical changes.", "A candle shows physical and chemical changes together.", "Some changes are reversible, some not; some desirable, some not.", "**Weathering** forms soil from rocks; **erosion** moves material by wind and water over thousands of years."])
